# Ordering — every spelling of "put these in order", and what each does with
# `missing`, `NaN`, and signed zeros.
#   helix run examples/language/ordering.helix
#
# Every `>>>` below is executed on the tree-walker, the bytecode VM and the JIT every
# time the gate runs, so this file cannot drift from what the language does.

## One order, one domain. `sort`, `argsort` and `sort_by` are three spellings of the same
## question, so they answer it the same way — including for DNA, and including when the
## array has `missing` (ADR 0025).
##
##     >>> [3, 1, 2].sort()
##     [1, 2, 3]
##
##     >>> [3, 1, 2].argsort()
##     [1, 2, 0]
##
##     >>> [3, 1, 2].sort_by(it)
##     [1, 2, 3]
##
##     >>> [dna("GG"), dna("AA")].sort()
##     [AA, GG]
##
##     >>> [dna("GG"), dna("AA")].argsort()
##     [1, 0]

## `missing` has no place in an order, so sorting REFUSES rather than inventing one — and
## says how to proceed. Dropping is an explicit, visible step (ADR 0001).
##
##     >>> [1, missing, 3].sort()
##     error: cannot sort: the array has missing values
##
##     >>> [1, missing, 3].argsort()
##     error: cannot sort: the array has missing values
##
##     >>> [1, missing, 3].drop_missing().sort()
##     [1, 3]

## `min`/`max` REDUCE, and a reduction over an array containing `missing` is `missing` —
## the three-valued answer, not an error. This is a different question from sorting, and
## the two give different answers on purpose.
##
##     >>> [3, 1, 2].min()
##     1
##
##     >>> [1, missing, 3].min()
##     missing
##
##     >>> [1, missing, 3].max()
##     missing

## NaN SORTS LAST, whatever its sign. A NaN is a Float value meaning "this computation
## failed" — it is not missing data, and nothing in Helix converts one into the other.
## `sqrt(-1.0)` happens to produce a NaN with its sign bit set on x86; that is invisible
## from Helix and does not change where it sorts.
##
##     >>> [1.0, sqrt(0.0 - 1.0), 3.0].sort()
##     [1.0, 3.0, NaN]
##
##     >>> [1.0, nan, 3.0].sort()
##     [1.0, 3.0, NaN]

## Sorting and COMPARING are different questions, and they answer differently on purpose.
## A sort has to put every value somewhere, so it puts NaN last. A comparison has to
## answer a Bool, and for a NaN there is no honest one — so it raises, and tells you the
## guard to write.
##
##     >>> [1.0, nan, 3.0].filter(x => x > 2.0)
##     error: cannot compare these values (NaN?)
##
##     >>> [1.0, nan, 3.0].filter(x => not is_nan(x)).filter(x => x > 2.0)
##     [3.0]

## `is_nan` and `is_missing` ask different questions, and a value answers `true` to at
## most one of them.
##
##     >>> nan.is_missing()
##     false
##
##     >>> missing.is_nan()
##     missing

## SIGNED ZEROS: `sort` orders them (`-0.0` before `0.0`), while the `_by` family and the
## `argmin`/`argmax` METHODS mirror the `<` operator, which is IEEE — under IEEE
## `-0.0 < 0.0` is false, so ties keep their original order and the FIRST one wins.
## The consequence is real and worth knowing: `xs[xs.argmin()]` and `xs.min()` can be
## different elements of `xs`. ADR 0025 (d) records this as a documented consequence
## that resolves when the comparators unify.
##
##     >>> [0.0, -0.0].sort()
##     [-0.0, 0.0]
##
##     >>> [0.0, -0.0].argmin()
##     0
##
##     >>> [-0.0, 0.0].argmin()
##     0
##
##     >>> [0.0, -0.0].min()
##     -0.0

print("sorted:", [3, 1, 2].sort(), " indices:", [3, 1, 2].argsort())
print("min of a column with a hole:", [1, missing, 3].min())
print("signed-zero tie, first wins:", [0.0, -0.0].argmin())
